    "description": "# Future Directions: Cryptography from Iterated Collatz Maps\n\nThis cycle produced `CollatzHashSecurity.lean`, which sharpens the catalog module\n`Cryptography.CollatzOWF` into a precise structural account of the candidate\none-way function `f(a, n) = T^a(n)`. The new results are: the exact one-step\ninversion law (`step_preimage_iff`), the two-to-one preimage bound and its full\nexponential generalization (`preimage_card_le_two`, `preimage_card_le_pow`),\nsurjectivity onto the positives with exponentially distant preimages\n(`f_surjective_pos`, `section_distance`), and \u2014 most importantly \u2014 an\n*unconditional, closed-form* collision family showing the raw integer iterate is\n**not** collision-resistant at any depth (`persistent_collision`,\n`f_not_injective`), with a matching boundary case (`f_zero_injective`) proving the\nsecurity parameter is essential.\n\nThe picture that emerges is a clean dichotomy: `T` is a genuine *one-way\ncandidate* (surjective, exponentially branching preimage tree) but a *broken hash*\nin the raw model, because explicit collisions exist for free. The directions below\nchase the question this raises: **what quotient or twist of `T` recovers collision\nresistance while keeping the exponential inversion barrier?**\n\n---\n\n## Direction 1 \u2014 Tight lower bound on the depth-`a` preimage count\n\nWe proved the preimage tree has `\u2264 2^a` leaves (`preimage_card_le_pow`). The\nsharper question is the *average* and *typical* branching: define\n`branch(a, c) = |{n : T^a(n) = c}|` and study its distribution. The key insight\nis that `preimage_card_le_pow` is an *envelope*, but the true count is governed by\nhow often the odd preimage `(c-1)/3` is itself odd along the backward tree \u2014 a\npurely congruential condition mod powers of 3. Conjecture: the expected number of\ndepth-`a` preimages of a random residue is `\u0398((4/3)^a)`, not `2^a`, because only a\n`1/3` fraction of nodes admit the odd branch. **Why now?** Our exact inversion law\n`step_preimage_iff` reduces the entire backward tree to a deterministic mod-2/mod-3\nrecursion, so the branching factor is now a finite-state Markov chain on residues\nthat Lean can model and bound \u2014 no Collatz convergence needed.\n\n## Direction 2 \u2014 Collision resistance of the *modular* hash `H_k(x) = T^k(x) mod 2^m`\n\nOur `persistent_collision` kills injectivity of the raw map, but the practical\nproposal was always the *reduced* hash `H_k(x) = T^k(x) mod 2^m`. The key insight\nis that reduction mod `2^m` interacts with the even branch `T(2n) = n` in a\ncontrolled way: halving is a *shift*, so `H` essentially streams parity bits, and\nits collisions correspond to inputs whose parity vectors agree on the low `m` bits\nafter `k` steps. Conjecture (falsifiable): for `k \u2265 m`, the family `8, 1` and every\nother \"short tail\" collision of the raw map is *destroyed* by the mod-`2^m`\nreduction once `k` exceeds the 2-adic valuation gap, so `H_k` regains injectivity\non `[0, 2^m)` for `k` in an explicit window. **Why now?** The raw collisions we\nexhibited are all generated by the even-branch section `n \u21a6 2n`; tracking their\n2-adic valuation under reduction is elementary arithmetic that the proven\n`step_double` and `iter_double` lemmas already encode.\n\n## Direction 3 \u2014 A provably one-way *padded* iterate immune to the `8, 1` attack\n\nThe collision `8 \u2192 4 \u2190 1` exists because two trajectories *merge*. The key insight\nis that merging is exactly the failure of injectivity of `T`, and one can restore\ninjectivity by carrying a *parity log*: define `T\u0302(n) = (T(n), n mod 2)` on\n`\u2115 \u00d7 Bool`, recording the bit that was consumed. Conjecture: `T\u0302^a` is injective on\nits reachable set for every `a` (no collisions, ever), while still being as hard to\ninvert as `T^a` because recovering `n` from `(T^a(n), bits)` requires reconstructing\nthe trajectory. This trades a `2^a`-leaf preimage tree for a *unique* path, turning\nthe one-way candidate into a genuine one-way *permutation* on a structured domain.\n**Why now?** Our `step_preimage_iff` shows the lost information at each step is\n*exactly one bit* (which branch was taken), so the padding is canonical and its\ninjectivity is provable by the same parity case-split we already automated.\n\n## Direction 4 \u2014 Quantitative inversion hardness in a query model\n\nWe have an exponential *combinatorial* barrier (`preimage_card_le_pow`), but not a\nhardness *theorem* in a computational model. The key insight is that the backward\ntree is the only structure an inverter can exploit, so in an oracle model where the\nadversary may only evaluate `T` forward, finding a preimage of a random target at\ndepth `a` requires expected `\u03a9((4/3)^a)` forward queries (cf. Direction 1's\nbranching constant). Conjecture: formalize a forward-only query game in Lean and\nprove a query lower bound matching the preimage-tree size, giving the first\n*unconditional* (model-relative) one-wayness statement for an iterated arithmetic\nmap. **Why now?** Mathlib now has enough probability and counting infrastructure to\nstate query lower bounds, and our preimage-counting lemmas supply the exact tree\nsize that such a bound must match.\n\n## Direction 5 \u2014 Cross-domain bridge: Collatz preimage trees as expander-like graphs\n\nThe catalog contains substantial expander/random-walk machinery\n(`Cryptography.ExpanderWalk`, `ClassicalGroupExpanders`). The key insight is that\nthe Collatz *backward* graph \u2014 vertices `\u2115`, edges from `c` to its `\u2264 2`\npreimages \u2014 is a bounded-degree graph whose mixing properties would directly\ncontrol hash diffusion. Conjecture: the backward Collatz graph restricted to\n`[0, 2^m)` (with wraparound mod `2^m`) is an expander, and its spectral gap lower\nbounds the collision-resistance of `H_k` from Direction 2. This bridges dynamical\nirreversibility (this module) with spectral cryptography (the expander catalog).\n**Why now?** Our two-to-one bound `preimage_card_le_two` is precisely the\nbounded-degree hypothesis every expander argument starts from, so the graph is\nalready in scope \u2014 only the spectral estimate remains.\n",
